        "title": "Digital Humanities: Text Mining, Network Analysis, GIS in History",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Digital humanities (DH) applies computational methods to humanistic research. For historians, this means three main capabilities: reading large corpora that no single person could read (text mining); analyzing relational structures across thousands of documents (network analysis); and situating historical data in geographic space (GIS). The fundamental methodological tension is between distant reading (patterns in large datasets) and close reading (detailed interpretation of specific texts) -- a tension that maps onto the longstanding historiographical debate between structural and interpretive approaches."
    }
